FORWARD LEG ONLY, VIA CHANNEL ALLOWLIST. The forward path is built from your own
outgoing channels, so leaving exactly one open -- to @RELAY@ -- gives it one
candidate. The RETURN leg is untouched and still drawn weighted-random, which is
the point: against pin-planner this isolates return-leg striping on its own.

PREREQUISITE, DONE BY HAND, ONCE PER RELAY.

The allowlist governs which peers the strategy OPENS channels to. It does not
close the ones you already have, and neither does the strategy -- edgli opens and
tops up, it never closes. So before this arm means anything you have to close
your other outgoing channels yourself.

That is an on-chain operation on your Safe, not something this client can do:
gnosis_vpn-ctl has no channel commands at all. Use the Safe that owns the
channels (HoprChannels on Gnosis Chain). Closing returns the stake to the Safe --
you pay gas and wait out the closure period, you do not lose the funding, and you
do NOT need a new identity or a faucet code.

Order matters. Set the allowlist first, then close: with the allowlist already in
place the strategy will not reopen the ones you just closed.

VERIFY BEFORE TRUSTING IT:

  sudo ./bench/use-arm.sh <this arm> --count     # must report 1 route

A count above 1 means a channel you meant to close is still open, or the closure
period has not elapsed.

RUN IT AS ITS OWN STUDY. Not because of identity -- that was wrong -- but because
the channel set is a property of the NODE, not of the arm. While you are down to
one channel, 'auto' is not auto. Restore the channel set, re-baseline, then
compare.
